"americas-most-innovative-companies", "title": "America's Most Innovative Companies", "year": 2023, "rank": 4, "itemDescription": ""}, {"slug": "fortune500", "title": "Fortune 500", "year": 2023, "rank": 101, "itemDescription": ""}, {"slug": "global500", "title": "Fortune Global 500", "year": 2022, "rank": 348, "itemDescription": ""}, {"slug": "fortune500", "title": "Fortune 500", "year": 2022, "rank": 91, "itemDescription": "<span style=\"font-weight: 400\">Best known for its database software, the venerable Oracle has struggled in recent years with sluggish revenue growth: Total revenue was only 3.6% in 2021, and the company slid down 11 spots on the Fortune 500. In December 2021, the company took a bold step toward boosting that growth, announcing a $28 billion deal to acquire electronic health records company Cerner; as of May 2022, that deal was still under regulatory review in the U.S. and Europe. </span>"}, {"slug": "global500", "title": "Fortune Global 500", "year": 2021, "rank": 304, "itemDescription": "<span data-sheets-value=\"{&quot;1&quot;:2,&quot;2&quot;:&quot;The database giant is trying mightily to fight off stagnation. Sales dropped 1% year over year to $39.1 billion, owing in part to Oracle\u2019s cloud unit, which is growing far more slowly than those of rivals Amazon and Microsoft. Last fall, Oracle made a surprising push along with Walmart to acquire the U.S. operations of red-hot TikTok, in a deal championed by former President Donald Trump. But that deal is now reportedly on hold under the Biden administration. In another blow: The U.S. Supreme Court recently sided with Google parent Alphabet over Oracle in a major copyright infringement case involving the use of Java software code.&quot;}\" data-sheets-userformat=\"{&quot;2&quot;:12673,&quot;3&quot;:{&quot;1&quot;:0},&quot;10&quot;:2,&quot;11&quot;:4,&quot;15&quot;:&quot;Calibri&quot;,&quot;16&quot;:11}\">The database giant is trying mightily to fight off stagnation. Sales dropped 1% year over year to $39.1 billion, owing in part to Oracle\u2019s cloud unit, which is growing far more slowly than those of rivals Amazon and Microsoft. Last fall, Oracle made a surprising push along with Walmart to acquire the U.S. operations of red-hot TikTok, in a deal championed by former President Donald Trump. But that deal is now reportedly on hold under the Biden administration. In another blow: The U.S. Supreme Court recently sided with Google parent Alphabet over Oracle in a major copyright infringement case involving the use of Java software code.</span>"}, {"slug": "fortune500", "title": "Fortune 500", "year": 2021, "rank": 80, "itemDescription": "<span data-sheets-value=\"{&quot;1&quot;:2,&quot;2&quot;:&quot;The database giant is trying mightily to fight off stagnation. Sales dropped 1% year over year to $39.1 billion, owing in part to Oracle\u2019s cloud unit, which is growing far more slowly than those of rivals Amazon and Microsoft. Last fall, Oracle made a surprising push along with Walmart to acquire the U.S. operations of red-hot TikTok, in a deal championed by former President Donald Trump. But that deal is now reportedly on hold under the Biden administration. In another blow: The U.S. Supreme Court recently sided with Google parent Alphabet over Oracle in a major copyright infringement case involving the use of Java software code.&quot;}\" data-sheets-userformat=\"{&quot;2&quot;:12673,&quot;3&quot;:{&quot;1&quot;:0},&quot;10&quot;:2,&quot;11&quot;:4,&quot;15&quot;:&quot;Calibri&quot;,&quot;16&quot;:11}\">The database giant is trying mightily to fight off stagnation. Sales dropped 1% year over year to $39.1 billion, owing in part to Oracle\u2019s cloud unit, which is growing far more slowly than those of rivals Amazon and Microsoft. Last fall, Oracle made a surprising push along with Walmart to acquire the U.S. operations of red-hot TikTok, in a deal championed by former President Donald Trump. But that deal is now reportedly on hold under the Biden administration. In another blow: The U.S. Supreme Court recently sided with Google parent Alphabet over Oracle in a major copyright infringement case involving the use of Java software code.</span>"}, {"slug": "global500", "title": "Fortune Global 500", "year": 2020, "rank": 314, "itemDescription": "Oracle had a difficult latter half of 2019, punctuated by the death of co-CEO Mark Hurd in October. Hurd had played a crucial role in generating sales for the company, often serving as Oracle's public face. His death came amid an already challenging period for Oracle, which ended its fiscal 2019 with year-over-year sales flat, at $39.5 billion. The stagnant revenue underscored the obstacles the database giant has faced in recent years in keeping up with flourishing rivals like Amazon Web Services and Microsoft."}, {"slug": "fortune500", "title": "Fortune 500", "year": 2020, "rank": 82, "itemDescription": "Oracle had a difficult latter half of 2019, punctuated by the death of co-CEO Mark Hurd in October. Hurd had played a crucial role in generating sales for the company, often serving as Oracle's public face. His death came amid an already challenging period for Oracle, which ended its fiscal 2019 with year-over-year sales flat, at $39.5 billion. The stagnant revenue underscored the obstacles the database giant has faced in recent years in keeping up with flourishing rivals like Amazon Web Services and Microsoft."}, {"slug": "global500", "title": "Fortune Global 500", "year": 2019, "rank": 307, "itemDescription": ""}, {"slug": "fortune500", "title": "Fortune 500", "year": 2019, "rank": 81, "itemDescription": ""}, {"slug": "worlds-most-admired-companies", "title": "World's Most Admired Companies", "year": 2018, "rank": null, "itemDescription": ""}, {"slug": "global500", "title": "Fortune Global 500", "year": 2018, "rank": 302, "itemDescription": ""}, {"slug": "fortune500", "title": "Fortune 500", "year": 2018, "rank": 82, "itemDescription": ""}, {"slug": "global500", "title": 